import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 575 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 576 | 2026-10-20 | **Scales: the fingering lessons merged and shown as strips of groups (her call, 12:17 and 12:21).** Lessons 4 and 5 are now one lesson, `sc-finger` \"Fingering from a white key\": the standard shape (7 steps), then one strip of groups for every white-key start (C G D A E, F, B: right and left hand; F's right hand and B's left hand marked ≠, only to notice that they differ), then \"standard or different?\" (six pairs, both different ones always in). The old `sc-odd` lesson is removed. The black-key lesson (`sc-black`, now lesson 5) opens with a strip table: B♭ ·-3-4, E♭ ·-4-3, A♭ ··-3-3, D♭ ··-4-··, left hand 3-4-· for all four, i.e. the same groups of 3 and 4 in another order; then the thumb screens and her turn as before. New `ScStrip` (coral = group of 3, blue = group of 4, grey = the rest, finger numbers inside, read from her `SCALES` arrays). On the keyboards in lessons 3-6 black keys now carry both names (e.g. C♯ / D♭); finger numbers sit in one straight row above the keys with a ring round every thumb (`nums` on `ScKeys`). Lessons 1-2 keep their arrows and white-key labels. Concept now: 1 pattern, 2 same pattern, 3 degrees, 4 white-key fingering, 5 black-key fingering; next 6 all twelve keys, 7 chromatic, 8 the minors. Korean wording is mine, unverified. Built by `patch582.py` (`t582`). Checked in English and Korean, no errors. Local commit only, not pushed. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
